# Untitled

In-situ cation exchange enhances room temperature phosphorescence of a family of metal-organic frameworks
<sup>1</sup>College of Chemistry, State Key Laboratory of Elemento-Organic Chemistry, Nankai University, Tianjin 300071, China; <sup>2</sup>School of Materials Science and Engineering, Tianjin Key Laboratory of Metal and Molecule-Based Material Chemistry, Nankai University, Tianjin 300350, China
The rational designability and chemical tunability of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are enabling tributes to efficaciously enhance their room temperature phosphorescence (RTP) performance. A family of stable anionic MOFs, \(\mathrm{[Zn_{2}(4,5-ImDC)_{2}]M_{2}}\) (NKU-132, \(\mathrm{M}=(\mathrm{CH})_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{(CH_{2}CH_{3})_{2}NH_{2}}\)), featuring significant RTP have been synthesized. By rational cation selection and in-situ replacement from dimethylammonium to diethylammonium, the phosphorescence lifetime is increased from 30.88 to \(126.3~\mathrm{ms}\), along with less sensitivity to air. This work provides an anti-quenching and lifetime tuning example for RTP-MOF materials via facile host-guest chemistry.
metal-organic framework, room temperature phosphorescence, crystal engineering, in situ regulation
Citation: Feng R, Li ZY, Yao ZQ, Guo ZA, Zhang YN, Sun HX, Li W, Bu XH. In-situ cation exchange enhances room temperature phosphorescence of a family of metal-organic frameworks. Sci China Chem, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11426-021-1123-3
## 1 Introduction
Room temperature phosphorescence (RTP) materials based on long-lived triplet excitons have received widespread attention due to their various application potentials [1–5] in display technology [6–8], photovoltaics [9,10], bioimaging [11,12] and sensing [13,14]. In recent years, purely organic RTP materials have become a desirable platform for conquering the limited variety, hard machinability and high cost of traditional inorganic phosphorescent materials [15–20]. To achieve efficient RTP in these organic materials, the aggregation is needed to reduce the quenching and non-radiative decay rate. However, the simple condensation of RTP molecules often suffers from energy loss via quenching and hence inevitably limits the phosphorescence performance [21,22]. To overcome this drawback, assembling organic RTP ligands with metal nodes to form metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) could be an effective strategy as the formation of rigid framework can largely reduce the lattice vibration destructive for radiative recombination. More importantly, the facile structural tunability of MOFs enables the possible manipulation of RTP at a molecular level. The directionality of the coordination space makes it convenient to build rigid structures by considering the forming topology, which can effectively lower energy loss of non-radiative transition and reduce interference of excitons. More importantly, the microporous cavities or channels in MOFs provide additional degree of freedom to manage the RTP performance upon host-guest interactions (Scheme 1). At present, there have been various reports of RTP-MOFs [23–26], whereas the regulation of phosphorescence properties of MOFs is lack of investigation [27–29].
RTP ligands with metal nodes to form metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) could be an effective strategy as the formation of rigid framework can largely reduce the lattice vibration destructive for radiative recombination. More importantly, the facile structural tunability of MOFs enables the possible manipulation of RTP at a molecular level. The directionality of the coordination space makes it convenient to build rigid structures by considering the forming topology, which can effectively lower energy loss of non-radiative transition and reduce interference of excitons. More importantly, the microporous cavities or channels in MOFs provide additional degree of freedom to manage the RTP performance upon host-guest interactions (Scheme 1). At present, there have been various reports of RTP-MOFs [23– 26], whereas the regulation of phosphorescence properties of MOFs is lack of investigation [27–29].
Indeed, guests have been proved to be a significant factor in influencing properties and functionalities of MOFs. In
RTP MOFs, guests can be used to manipulate the energy level by participating in the initial photophysical process [30,31] and change the intensity of non-radiative transitions [32,33]. Since the guest molecule can influence the phosphorescence lifetime and intensity by modulating the photophysical processes and efficiency, how to tune the interactions between the framework and guests is critically important. However, there are very few examples of intensifying phosphorescence performance of MOFs by manipulating guests. The guests-filled frameworks lead to a denser packing which could significantly decrease the probability of interference between adjacent phosphors. In addition, the guest-filling could effectively minimize the energy loss induced by lattice vibration and rotation of chemical bonds and exclude the attacking of quenching molecules.
To improve RTP performance of MOFs, close-packed structures are desirable because they could effectively reduce the non-radiative recombination. Since the phosphorescence group usually contains aromatic rings, it is challenging to fit them on the apertures completely. In this context, constructing an ultramicroporous phosphorescence backbone with suitable ions would be an applicable approach, which proposes requirements on both the topology and size of nodes. Basically, the topology types with simple structure are more suitable, i.e., MOFs with the transitivity representation [1111] such as bcu, dia, nba, pcu and srs would be most applicable candidates ([pqr] represents for topology transitivity of MOFs. p: kinds of vertex; q: edge; r: face; s: tile. See more information on https://rcsr.net/). Moreover, by introducing N atoms, the cavity created by ligands could be further reduced. Considering the above requirements, 4,5-imidazolate dicarboxylic acid (4,5-ImDC) ligand would be a suitable phosphorescence ligand.
By rational selection of topology, rigid ligands and single metal nodes, a guest-tunable RTP-MOF \([\mathrm{Zn}(4,5-\mathrm{ImDC})]\cdot[(\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}]\) (denoted as NKU-132-95%DMA, DMA = dimethylammonium) was designed. To modify the filling rate, the framework guests were in-situ replaced from DMA to diethylammonium (DEA) by the solvent change from \(N,N\)-dimethylformamide (DMF) to \(N,N\)-dimethylacetamide (DMAC) and \(N,N\)-diethylformamide (DEF) (denoted as NKU-132-80%DMA and NKU-132-70%DEA, respectively). NKU-132-70%DEA shows anti-quenching effects to air and exhibits the most extended lifetime of \(126.3~\mathrm{ms}\) at RT, which was estimated as \(30.88~\mathrm{ms}\) for NKU-132-95%DMA and \(19.21~\mathrm{ms}\) for 4,5-ImDC.
