## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Materials and instruments
1H-imidazole-4,5-dicarboxylic acid (4,5-ImDC), \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}\cdot6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), fluoroboric acid and all other reagents and solvents were commercially available and used without further purification. Single-crystal X-ray diffraction (XRD) test was produced on a Bruker D8 VENTURE (Germany) with Ga Kα \((\lambda=0.134139\;\mathrm{nm})\). The crystal was kept at \(192.97\;\mathrm{K}\) during data collection. The structure was solved with Olex2 and ShelXT structure solution program using intrinsic phasing and refined with the ShelXL refinement package using least squares minimization. Thermogravimetric analysis was tested on Rigaku Thermo Plus EVO2 analyzer (Japan). Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy was measured by Bruker Tensor 37 spectrometer (Germany). Powder XRD tests were produced by Rigaku MiniFlex 600 diffractometer (Japan). Fluorescence and phosphorescence spectra were experimented on a Hitachi F7000 fluorescence spectrophotometer (Japan). Phosphorescence decay curves were experimented on an Edinburgh FLS980 fluorescence spectrometer (UK). Quantum yields were estimated on an Edinburgh FLS900 fluorescence spectrometer (UK). The topology structure was characterized by ToposPro package [34]. The Commission International del'Eclairage (CIE) coordinates were calculated by CIE1931xy program. CCDC No. of reported structures: 2096709-11.
2.2 Synthesis of \(\left[(\mathbf{NH}_{2}\mathbf{Me})_{2_{0.95}}(\mathbf{H}_{3}\mathbf{O})_{0.05}\mathbf{Zn}\left(\mathbf{ImDC}\right)\right]\cdot\left(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}\right)_{0.3}\cdot\mathbf{DMF}_{0.05}\) (NKU-132-95%DMA)
A mixture of 4,5-ImDC (15.6 mg, 0.1 mmol), \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{6}\cdot6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (29.7 mg, 0.1 mmol) was dissolved in 3 mL solvent with molar ratio of \(\mathrm{DMF/EtOH/H_{2}O}=2{:}0.5{:}0.5\), and \(50\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) fluoroboric acid was added into the mixture subsequently. The mixture was put in a 5 mL glass vial and heated at \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 3 days, followed by slow cooling \((0.5\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\,\mathrm{min}^{-1})\) to room temperature. Colorless polyhedral crystals were obtained and washed by DMF and EtOH for 3 times, respectively. The solids were dried at \(80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(12\,\mathrm{h}\) (yield: 60 % based on ligand). Infrared spectroscopy (IR) \((\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), KBr, Figure S1, Supporting Information online): 3,407 \((\nu_{\mathrm{N-H}},\,\mathrm{m},\,\mathrm{br})\), 3,043 (s), 2,961 (s), 2,785 (s), 2,466 (m), 1,577 \((\gamma_{\mathrm{N-H}},\,\mathrm{s},\,\mathrm{br})\), 1,473 \((\delta_{\mathrm{CH}_{3}},\,\mathrm{s})\), 1,392 \((\delta_{\mathrm{CH}_{3}},\,\mathrm{s})\), 1,297 (m), 1,250 (s), 1192 (w), 1,103 \((\nu_{\mathrm{C-N}},\,\mathrm{s})\), 1,022 (m), 855 (m), 817 \((\beta_{\mathrm{N-H}},\,\mathrm{s})\), 799 (s), 669 (m), 533 (m), 460 (w). Anal. Calculated for
\(\mathrm{ZnC_{7.2}H_{11.05}N_{3.05}O_{4.45}}\): C, 31.23; H, 4.002; N, 15.43. Found: C, 31.32; H, 3.993; N, 15.29.
2.3 Synthesis of \(\left[(\mathbf{NH}_{2}\mathbf{Me})_{0.8}(\mathbf{H}_{3}\mathbf{O})_{0.2}\mathbf{Zn}(\mathbf{ImDC})\right]\) \(\mathbf{\cdotDMAC_{0.1}}\) (NKU-132-80%DMA)
The synthesis method for NKU-132-80%DMA was similar with NKU-132-95%DMA except the solvent and molar ratio was changed into \(\mathrm{DMAC/CH_{3}CN/H_{2}O=2:0.5:0.5}\). Colorless polyhedral crystals were obtained and washed by DMA and EtOH for 3 times, respectively. The solids were dried at \(80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(12\,\mathrm{{h}}\) (yield: \(46\%\) based on ligand). IR \((\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), KBr, Figure S1): 3,421 \((\nu_{\mathrm{N-H}},\,\mathrm{m},\,\mathrm{br})\), 3,043 (s), 2,962 (s), 2,784 (s), 2,464 (m), 1,576 \((\nu_{\mathrm{N-H}},\,\mathrm{s},\,\mathrm{br})\), 1,473 \((\delta_{\mathrm{CH3}},\,\mathrm{s})\), 1,392 \((\delta_{\mathrm{CH3}},\,\mathrm{s})\), 1,298 (m), 1,250 (s), 1,193 (m), 1,103 \((\nu_{\mathrm{C-N}},\,\mathrm{s})\), 1,023 (m), 854 (m), 817 \((\beta_{\mathrm{N-H}},\,\mathrm{s})\), 800 (m), 669 (m), 533 (m), 459 (w). Anal. Calculated for \(\mathrm{ZnC_{8}H_{9}N_{2.9}O_{4.3}}\): C, 31.27; H, 3.693; N, 15.11. Found: C, 31.26; H, 3.625; N, 15.36.
2.4 Synthesis of \(\left[(\mathbf{NH}_{2}\mathbf{Et})_{0.7}(\mathbf{H}_{3}\mathbf{O})_{0.3}\mathbf{Zn}(\mathbf{ImDC})\right]\cdot\mathbf{DEF}_{0.1}\) (NKU-132-70%DEA)
The synthesis method for NKU-132-70%DEA was similar with NKU-132-95%DMA except DMF was replaced by DEF. Colorless polyhedral crystals were obtained and washed by DEF and EtOH for 3 times, respectively. The solids were dried at \(80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(12\,\mathrm{{h}}\) (yield: \(52\,\%\) based on ligand). IR \((\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), KBr, Figure S1): 3,400 \((\nu_{\mathrm{N-H}},\,\mathrm{w})\), 2,987 (s), 2,823 (s, br), 2,498 (m), 1,558 \((\nu_{\mathrm{N-H}},\,\mathrm{s},\,\mathrm{dd})\), 1,472 \((\delta_{\mathrm{CH3}},\,\mathrm{s})\), 1,387 \((\delta_{\mathrm{CH3}},\,\mathrm{s})\), 1,296 (m), 1,247 (s), 1,169 (m), 1,102 \((\nu_{\mathrm{C-N}},\,\mathrm{s})\), 1,013 (m), 857 (m), 826 \((\beta_{\mathrm{N-H}},\,\mathrm{s})\), 796 (s), 666 (s), 530 (m), 450 (w). Anal. Calculated for \(\mathrm{ZnC_{8.3}H_{12.4}N_{2.8}O_{4.4}}\): C, 34.72; H, 4.332; N, 13.66. Found: C, 34.62; H, 4.056; N, 13.59.
## 2.5 Calculations of electronic structures
The total density of state (TDOS), partial density of states (PDOS) and the crystal orbitals (including the highest occupied crystal orbital (HOCO) and the lowest unoccupied crystal orbital (LUCO)) were calculated by Vienna ab initio simulation package (VASP). Perdew-Burke-Ernzerhof (PBE) generalized gradient approximation (GGA) with Grimme's DFT-D3 correction was selected to perform geometry relaxation and subsequent calculations [35–38]. The cutoff energy of the plane wave basis set was \(550\;\mathrm{eV}\), and Brillouin zone was sampled by a \(2\times2\times2\) gamma-centered mesh. The geometry was optimized until the forces acting on atoms were below \(0.01\;\mathrm{eV}\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1}\), and the energy convergence criterion was set to \(10^{-6}\,\mathrm{eV}\). The calculation of orbitals and DOS was performed in the same level, whereas a \(k\)-point mesh of \(5\times5\times5\) was used for DOS calculation. VESTA package was used for generation of input files and partial charge density plot with isosurface level of 0.05 [39].
## 3 Results and discussion
## 3.1 Crystal structure
NKU-132-95%DMA crystallizes in the cubic \(P_{2,3}\) space group with \(a=9.9843(3)\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\). In the asymmetric unit, there are one \(\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}\) and one 4,5-ImDC. Each Zn atom is 6-coordinated by three 4,5-ImDC ligands, and each 4,5-ImDC coordinates with three Zn atoms forming an anionic \([\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(4,5\mathrm{-}\mathrm{ImDC})_{2}]^{2-}\) framework structure (Figure 1a). The framework has square pores along the 2-fold screw axis and triangular pores along the 3-fold rotation axis (Figure 1b, d). By simplifying 4,5-ImDC and Zn to 3-connected nodes, the NKU-132 framework has a point symbol of \([10^{3}]\) and srs topology (Figure 1c, and Figure S2). In the framework channels, DMA cations decomposed from DMF are located, also with a 3-fold disordered form (Figure 1d). NKU-132-95%DMA retains stable up to \(300\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Figure S3) and in common solvents including water with pH 3–12 (Figures S4 and S5).
## 3.2 Luminescence properties
When exposed to a \(365\;\mathrm{nm}\) ultraviolet (UV) light under ambient conditions, NKU-132-95%DMA emits light blue fluorescence (Figure S6) and short cyan afterglow after exposure, which is difficult to be observed with naked eyes. To change the occupation ratio of aperture for a better performance, DMAC and DEF were introduced in the synthetic procedure to substitute DMF (Figure S7, denoted as NKU-132-95%DMA, NKU-132-80%DMA, and NKU-132-70% DEA, respectively). Compared with DMF, DMAC is less inclined to decompose to DMA cations than DMF in \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\)
and acidic solution, further increasing the degree of freedom of the cavity. In contrast, DEF can provide larger DEA cations, which increases the rigidity of inner molecules and occupancy of apertures.
The 4,5-ImDC ligand (Figure S8) presents emission peaks at \( 465 \, \mathrm{nm} \) of fluorescence and \( 486 \, \mathrm{nm} \) for phosphorescence, respectively, which correspond to the CIE coordinates at (0.154, 0.176) and (0.174, 0.325). Compared with the ligand, NKU-132-95%DMA possesses a fluorescence peak at \( 430 \, \mathrm{nm} \) and a phosphorescence peak at \( 470 \, \mathrm{nm} \), showing a blueshift effect (Figure 2a, e). NKU-132-80%DMA has a fluorescence peak at \( 441 \, \mathrm{nm} \) and phosphorescence peak at \( 466 \, \mathrm{nm} \), and NKU-132-70%DEA exhibits corresponding peaks at 399 and \( 482 \, \mathrm{nm} \), respectively (Figures S9 and S10). It should be noticed that the fluorescence peak is blueshifted in the sequence of NKU-132-80%DMA, NKU-132-95%DMA, and NKU-132-70%DEA. Conversely, the peaks of phosphorescence are redshifted in the same order (Figure 2e, Table S3, Supporting Information online). However, the quantum yields increase as the order of NKU-132-95%DMA, \( 80\% \) DMA and \( 70\% \) DEA, which are \( 0.94\% \), \( 1.25\% \), and \( 1.45\% \), respectively. The UV-visible (Vis) spectra of NKU-132 family compounds show similar adsorption peaks at approximately \( 350 \, \mathrm{nm} \) and the bandgap is calculated to be \( 3.13 \, \mathrm{eV} \) for NKU-132-95%DMA (Figure S11).
The phosphorescence decay process is significantly influenced by the filling guests, which could be quantified by the lifetime measurements (Figure 2f). The NKU-132-80%DMA reveals a shorter lifetime \( (6.518 \, \mathrm{ms}) \) than that of 4,5-ImDC \( (19.21 \, \mathrm{ms}) \), while NKU-132-95%DMA and \( 70\% \) DEA show longer lifetimes \( (30.88 \, \mathrm{ms} \) and \( 126.3 \, \mathrm{ms} \), respectively, Figure S12) than that of the ligand. The fluorescence pho
tographs of NKU-132-95%DMA, NKU-132-80%DMA and NKU-132-70%DEA crystals under \(365\;\mathrm{nm}\) UV excitation are shown in Figure 3 (Video S1, Supporting Information online). It is interesting that NKU-132-80%DMA shows a longer afterglow than NKU-132-95%DMA, which can be attributed to a higher quantum yield. The cyan-color afterglow of NKU-132-70%DMA can still be observed after \(3.0\;\mathrm{s}\) by naked eye. Images of afterglow were also captured in the argon atmosphere simultaneously. NKU-132-80%DMA shows a smaller difference of decay time between air and argon than that of NKU-132-95%DMA. However, NKU-132-70%DEA shows almost same decay time in the two different atmospheres, which indicates an anti-quenching effect due to the existence of oxygen in air (Video S2). The temperature-dependent fluorescence and phosphorescence spectra of NKU-132 series demonstrate that NKU-132-80%DMA displays the highest reduction of phosphorescence intensity upon heating from 240 to \(340\;\mathrm{K}\) (Figure S13). At \(323\;\mathrm{K}\), NKU-132-70%DMA still exhibits afterglow, whereas NKU-132-95%DMA and \(80\%\mathrm{DMA}\) lose their phosphorescence completely (Figure S14). The high temperature responsibility of phosphorescence indicates NKU-132 could be used as phosphorescence switches at ambient and even higher temperature.
## 3.3 Mechanism
The improvement of phosphorescence lifetime for NKU-132-95%DMA in the ambient environment compared with \(4,5\mathrm{-ImDC}\) can be ascribed to the enhanced ligand rigidity enabled by the anionic framework and counter cations, which substantially decreases the energy loss of non-radiative transition. The in-situ change of homologue solvents is proved to be a suitable solution to tune RTP, which could lead to improved anti-quenching effect, lifetime, and quantum yields. As shown from the Jablonski diagram (Figure 4c), the phosphorescence lifetime \(\tau_{\mathrm{p}}\) is expressed by the following formula:
\[
\tau_{\mathrm{p}}=\frac{1}{k_{\mathrm{q}}+k_{\mathrm{nr}}+k_{\mathrm{p}}}
\]
where \(k_{\mathrm{q}}\) is the overall quenching rate of \(\mathrm{T}_{1}\), \(k_{\mathrm{nr}}\) is the triplet non-radiative decay rate, and \(k_{\mathrm{p}}\) is the phosphorescent decay rate. As a linear microporous MOF, a guest molecule with appropriate configuration for specific apertures could lead to a lower \(k_{\mathrm{nr}}\) and hence reduce \(k_{\mathrm{q}}\). However, the crystal structure reveals that dimethylammonium cannot occupy the whole apertures and prevent quenching effects. NKU-132-95%DMA behaves differently between air and argon atmo
sphere, indicating a quenching process by oxygen molecules. In contrast, NKU-132-80%DMA shows a less significant quenching effect due to the occupation of solvent molecules in remained spaces by smaller cations (such as \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O}^{+}\)cations), and NKU-132-70%DEA shows the best anti-quenching effect with suitable size matching property. In addition, the vibration of framework determines the intensity of the non-radiation transition, which can be reflected by the equivalent isotropic atomic displacement parameter \((U_{\mathrm{eq}})\) of the framework. The \(U_{\mathrm{eq}}\) of Zn atoms are 0.0984(7), 0.1058(7) and 0.0796(7) for NKU-132-95%DMA, NKU-132-80%DMA, and NKU-132-70%DEA, respectively, which reveals that \(k_{\mathrm{nr}}\) increases in the order of NKU-132-70%DEA, NKU-132-95%DMA, and NKU-132-80%DMA. Furthermore, the \(k_{\mathrm{p}}\) is defined in the following formula:
\[
k_{\mathrm{p}}=\frac{64\pi^3}{3\hbar^4c^2}\Delta E_{\mathrm{T_1}\rightarrow\mathrm{S_0}}^3\left|\mu_{\mathrm{T_1}\rightarrow\mathrm{S_0}}\right|^2
\]
where \(\Delta E\) and \(\mu\) are the energy difference and transition dipole moment between \(\mathrm{T}_{1}\) and \(\mathrm{S}_{0}\). \(\Delta E_{\mathrm{T}_{1}-\mathrm{S}_{0}}\) can be directly observed by the phosphorescence spectra, which indicates the phosphorescence decay rate of NKU-132-80%DMA is faster than that of NKU-132-95%DMA, whereas the phosphorescence decay rate of NKU-132-70%DEA is the lowest. Among three compounds, NKU-132-70%DMA shows the lowest \(k_{\mathrm{q}}\) and \(k_{\mathrm{nr}}\) and low \(k_{\mathrm{q}}\), which gives rise to the best phosphorescence lifetime and quantum yield. Although NKU-132-80%DMA possesses the lowest lifetime, its moderate anti-quenching performance and a relatively high \(k_{\mathrm{p}}\) result in a higher quantum yield compared with NKU-132-95%DMA.
## 4 Conclusions
In summary, the guest-tunable RTP was achieved in a family of MOFs, namely NKU-132 series. By in-situ guest replacement of a more compatible DEA cation, the RTP of NKU-132-70%DEA shows a fourfold-extended lifetime to 126.3 ms. This work opens up a new avenue to engineer RTP performance of MOFs via a host-guest approach, which could be also applicable to other types of framework materials.
